#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172083 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171889} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172084 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171890} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172088 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171894} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171897} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "280e7ba4-7617-546b-9e0d-a5bdc33dad8d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Simply-Connected Regions in Euclidian Space"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172130 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171710} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172131 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171711} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b529430-3406-506c-b67d-c067dfee44d5" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172282}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172292}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172378}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173164 :tutorial/content "A region $D$ is **connected** if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path that lies entirely in $D.$\n\nThe region below is connected because a smooth path can join any two points.\n\n![](images/90/90fea89e0dabec960edf7eef3552e10cdbccaee80f9ef6ca3d5b2a5b457e6b89.png)\n\nThe region below is not connected. If we take two points $P$ and $Q,$ one in each subregion, we cannot find a path joining these points that lies entirely in the region.\n\n![](images/08/08cd555c38106ec22f2ffe4808a6b62e8f72ffda7baf953836beeca5ee6a1faf.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "cc905518-dd4e-5791-9cf9-5655233a30a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5105 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173189 :tutorial/content "A connected region $D$ is **simply connected** if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nWhen we have a connected *plane* region (e.g. a region in the $xy$-plane), determining whether it's simply connected is a little simpler: we just need to check whether the region contains holes. \n\nPlane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nSo, if a connected plane region does not contain any holes, then it is simply connected.\n\nMore precisely, a connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a2c2be37-32a5-5b2c-8386-ef9e32684998" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5574 :tutorial/title "Simply-Connected Regions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173197 :tutorial/content "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ assigns a vector to every point $(x,y)$ in a region $S\\subset \\mathbb R^2$. We can visualize this by plotting the corresponding vector at each point in the coordinate plane.\n\nAs an example, let's sketch the vector field $\\mathbf F$ at the points $(1,1), (-3,3),$ and $(4,-3),$ where $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\\mathbf F(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j}.$$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we first substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}F(1,1) & =i+j \\\\ F(-3,3) & =-3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ F(4,-3) & =4\\,i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors, placing the tail ends of each vector at its corresponding point. This gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/da/daea999f1a9bff587f0550e6674a39ae27dc9a2bb58e35877ed15626de0d9791.png)\n\nApplying the same method to more points in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we obtain the following visualization of our vector field:\n\n![](images/03/03402a7a7e1967b428c3657d6be90e4b1fd7ddd3c8ccaa11a69b0ff7cd566859.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ed5e3d1-f4e2-5773-bbd0-5ef02d8b726d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5763 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
